The IDNA library's suite (tests/idna.scala) holds src/native/idn2/idna.c to GNU libidn2 2.3.8's answers.

expected.txt  host names and what GNU libidn2 2.3.8's idn2_to_ascii_8z answers for each with the flags 0: under
              `# <group>`, a row `<name>\t<code>\t<output>`, the name and the output as the hex of their bytes
              (a name may be ill-formed UTF-8), the output only where the code is 0. It is GNU's answers, never
              the library's: 2,873 rows in 58 groups, 628 of them converted, the others refused with each of the
              15 error codes GNU gives on the whole table
driver.c      what the suite links the library with: it converts the names of a table in one run

The answers were recorded outside the repository: GNU libidn2 2.3.8, built from its release tarball for
x86_64-linux-musl, linked statically into a program that converts each name with the flags 0. They are a sample of a
table of 60,347,346 names in 58 groups, on every row of which the library answers as GNU does, return code and
output alike. The sample is drawn with a fixed seed: in each group up to three rows of each return code and twelve
more at random; in the groups that put one code point in a context, also up to two rows of each pair of the code
point's UTS #46 status and the return code, and the rows of the four deviation characters (U+00DF, U+03C2, U+200C,
U+200D). Each sampled name was converted again and its answer is the one the whole table's run gave.

The groups, by the start of their names:
  01, 02  the strings of UTS #46's IdnaTestV2.txt, 15.1.0 and 18.0.0
  03      ASCII: dots, hyphens, `xn--` shapes, every pair of ASCII characters, alone and beside a non-ASCII letter
  04-cp   every code point in 34 contexts: alone, twice, upper-cased neighbours, between Latin, Hebrew and Arabic
          letters, beside European and Arabic digits, in a label of a name with a right-to-left label, around the
          joiners and after a virama, after `xn--`, among hyphens and dots, before combining marks, after
          precomposed letters and Hangul
  05      the strings of UAX #15's NormalizationTest.txt, 15.1.0
  06      Punycode: `xn--` labels short and long, valid and not, and the A-label of every code point alone and
          after `a`
  07      lengths of labels and names; 12 and 16: which length error a long name gets, before and after mapping
  08      ill-formed UTF-8
  09      real names, addresses, top-level domains, random names of letters, digits and hyphens
  10      emoji and mixed scripts; 11: random names of scripts' letters, characters with rules, any code point
  13, 14  bidi and joiner sequences; 15: which error a name with several gets
  20      GNU's own outputs fed back: as they are, upper-cased, truncated, extended, a letter changed
